WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.529
Before we begin our class, we are going

2
00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:05.169
to take a look at our Blueprint.

3
00:00:05.169 --> 00:00:07.950
So let's go ahead and do that now.

4
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.550
Now, first things first, Blueprint

5
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.910
has a flow direction or a flow control.

6
00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.190
And this goes from left to right,

7
00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.829
just like you read in English.

8
00:00:18.829 --> 00:00:22.870
So if we start right here, our flow will always

9
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.510
or execution will always go from here this way.

10
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.969
And we will continue like that throughout our graph.

11
00:00:29.969 --> 00:00:34.420
Now, there are a few ways that we can control this flow.

12
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.939
The most simplistic ways is going to be

13
00:00:36.939 --> 00:00:40.060
our branch or our if statement, which is right up here.

14
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.619
This works with a condition.

15
00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:44.219
We have something that comes in here.

16
00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:45.979
And then based off this condition,

17
00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.859
we're going to have a true or a false.

18
00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:49.979
So if something comes in here, if it's true,

19
00:00:49.979 --> 00:00:52.219
it's going to fire off the execution off of the true.

20
00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:55.740
If it's not true, it can fire the execution off of the false.

21
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.479
We can also have this condition

22
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.439
set to a variable, which will allow us to base

23
00:01:00.439 --> 00:01:03.719
the execution of this action off of other input

24
00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:06.239
in other areas of the project.

25
00:01:06.239 --> 00:01:07.920
Next, we have a flip-flop.

26
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.319
Flip-flops are really cool because of how they work.

27
00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:13.920
They will always do one input and then the other.

28
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.959
So when we get an input into a flip-flop,

29
00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:19.719
we can always be assured that it will fire A and then B

30
00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:22.439
and keep repeating this pattern.

31
00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:25.040
These are really good if you would like to maybe do something

32
00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:27.640
on button click, and then when you click it again,

33
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.599
it reverses that behavior or some other type of behavior

34
00:01:30.599 --> 00:01:33.480
that you know is going to have one output and then

35
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.700
another output.

36
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.000
Now, sequence nodes are useful in that they allow you

37
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.140
to fire off execution to a bunch of different parts

38
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.640
of your code all at one time.

39
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.680
Now, you can add additional VIN pins in your sequence

40
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.439
by pressing the Add Pin button right here.

41
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.840
And again, this will fire off a bunch of different executions.

42
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.599
It will do them in order that they

43
00:01:57.599 --> 00:01:59.120
come inside of the sequence.

44
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.159
So it will go then 0s, then 1, but there

45
00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:04.840
is no delay in the execution.

46
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.200
They will always fire from the top

47
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.759
and then go down to the bottom and fire off instantaneously.

48
00:02:11.759 --> 00:02:13.800
Next, we have the switch on int.

49
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.740
So switch on int allows us to provide an integer or an input

50
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.460
and then make a change based off of that.

51
00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.099
Switching on integer is a lot more useful

52
00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:26.819
than having to constantly remember

53
00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:30.780
the variable name or some other complicated name.

54
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.300
And simply having a number like 1, 2, 3, or 4

55
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.919
be able to change the output is super, super useful.

56
00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:42.060
Last but not least, we have a gate.

57
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.460
And a gate is used for executional-based conditional

58
00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:45.979
checking.

59
00:02:45.979 --> 00:02:49.560
So if we are trying to check for some type of condition,

60
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.479
while we are executing a piece of code,

61
00:02:51.479 --> 00:02:55.710
we could use a gate to set up the structure for that.

62
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.870
Now, we're not done just yet because Blueprints also

63
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.069
have a way to control time.

64
00:03:02.069 --> 00:03:03.810
Now, you might be thinking, well, I

65
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.150
could just make a timer.

66
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.069
If I have full execution control,

67
00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.509
I could probably get the game time.

68
00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:11.949
And yes, you could do that, but there

69
00:03:11.949 --> 00:03:15.349
are some built-in things that work way better.

70
00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:17.110
So first off, I want to talk about delays

71
00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:18.229
and retriggerable delays.

72
00:03:18.229 --> 00:03:21.469
Now, delays are a great little piece of code

73
00:03:21.469 --> 00:03:24.090
if you want to wait for a specific amount of time.

74
00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.750
In fact, if you put a delay of 0, 0,

75
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.030
that will act as a single-frame delay

76
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.069
inside of Blueprints.

77
00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:34.750
And this is actually utilized in Fortnite as well as

78
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:35.949
Robo Recall.

79
00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:38.150
Inside the Robo Recall Mall, they actually

80
00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.469
will pause stuff on a single frame to do calculations.

81
00:03:41.469 --> 00:03:43.509
And again, if you take a delay node

82
00:03:43.509 --> 00:03:45.270
and you put that duration to 0, 0,

83
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.949
it's that one-frame delay that's

84
00:03:46.949 --> 00:03:50.389
often needed to help things spawn in without twitching.

85
00:03:50.389 --> 00:03:52.349
Now, there's also a retriggerable delay,

86
00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:54.430
which will retrigger after a duration.

87
00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.430
Now, the problem with these delays

88
00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.069
are these are kind of fire and forget nodes.

89
00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:03.930
Once we fire these off, we have no control over them.

90
00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.590
We just kind of have to wait for them.

91
00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.509
So we set it, and we kind of wait for it to do its thing.

92
00:04:08.509 --> 00:04:10.349
And while that's good for many, many things,

93
00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:13.189
oftentimes that's not really good when we want precision.

94
00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:15.550
If we want precision, this is where

95
00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:20.389
the set timer by event and set timer by function name nodes

96
00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:22.980
come in real handy.

97
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.459
With these two nodes, we can set them

98
00:04:25.459 --> 00:04:30.660
to listen for a specific event or a specific function

99
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.779
and use this to help define how we keep track of time.

100
00:04:34.779 --> 00:04:36.899
One of the things to note on both of these nodes

101
00:04:36.899 --> 00:04:39.579
is both the time and the looping option.

102
00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:43.579
This allows us to basically create a timer of any duration,

103
00:04:43.579 --> 00:04:47.540
whether it's 0.1 seconds or 500 seconds.

104
00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.420
And we can even have that repeat some behavior

105
00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.279
on looping.

106
00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:54.779
So every time it counts maybe down for one second,

107
00:04:54.779 --> 00:04:56.660
we could have it loop and come back.

108
00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.740
So this is really going to work in our advantage

109
00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.980
as it's going to take a lot of the hard work of getting

110
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.100
our game time, converting that game time into a real time

111
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.540
that we could actually utilize to do things

112
00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.420
like keep track of our time.

113
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.980
Super, super simple and makes everything

114
00:05:12.019 --> 00:05:13.699
much more approachable.

115
00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:15.939
Now, the last node that we're going to talk about here

116
00:05:15.939 --> 00:05:17.300
is a timeline.

117
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:21.379
Now, a timeline is how you can animate objects inside

118
00:05:21.379 --> 00:05:22.300
of Blueprints.

119
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.779
Timelines will be added to the Blueprint graph,

120
00:05:24.779 --> 00:05:28.740
and they allow us to animate objects or properties of objects

121
00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.100
going from point A to point B in a linear fashion.

122
00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.379
While we aren't using timelines necessarily

123
00:05:34.379 --> 00:05:36.060
in this particular project, they

124
00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.100
are super useful to know about.

125
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.939
All right, this will finish up our Blueprints quick review

126
00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:44.180
We'll now move on to Unreal Motion

127
00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.579
Graphics in our next video.
